00:01:29.550 --> 00:01:30.925
What is the
developer experience?

00:01:30.925 --> 00:01:33.570
But also about how it's actually
architectured behind it so

00:01:33.570 --> 00:01:37.020
you can understand exactly
how it works and understand

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.480
how you could extend it.

00:01:39.480 --> 00:01:42.180
And finally, we're going to
do what the talk is about,

00:01:42.180 --> 00:01:44.190
serving your API to the world.

00:01:44.190 --> 00:01:47.010
And the world will be myself,
in this case, but you know,

00:01:47.010 --> 00:01:49.210
it's something.

00:01:49.210 --> 00:01:54.310
So let's start with,
what is an API?

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.810
I'm sure that you all
know what an API is.

00:01:56.810 --> 00:01:59.410
You've all used at
least once an API.

00:01:59.410 --> 00:02:02.230
And API's an Application
Programming Interface.

00:02:02.230 --> 00:02:04.450
And basically,
the whole point is

00:02:04.450 --> 00:02:09.580
that it allows other
programs use your programs.

00:02:09.580 --> 00:02:11.320
An API is not for
people to use it.

00:02:11.320 --> 00:02:12.834
It's for machines.

00:02:12.834 --> 00:02:13.750
That's pretty much it.

00:02:13.750 --> 00:02:16.020
So you could have a web page.

00:02:16.020 --> 00:02:21.280
We have a webpage serving
some data from a data store.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:24.340
And you can either
use the webpage--

00:02:24.340 --> 00:02:27.180
you're a human being and you
go in there click on buttons.

00:02:27.180 --> 00:02:28.684
But if you're a
program, you don't

00:02:28.684 --> 00:02:30.100
want to go there
clicking buttons,

00:02:30.100 --> 00:02:31.750
because then you
need to scrape HTML.

00:02:31.750 --> 00:02:34.660
And if you've ever done
that, it's not super fun

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:36.400
and also not always allowed.

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:40.270
So very often we
allow this other way

00:02:40.270 --> 00:02:41.950
of using the program,
same program,

00:02:41.950 --> 00:02:44.470
just a different
way of using it.

00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:48.180
Cool, so what is a REST API?

00:02:48.180 --> 00:02:51.870
I'm not going to try to
remember what REST stands for,

00:02:51.870 --> 00:02:53.760
because there's no
point and it really

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:56.580
doesn't mean that much,
at least in my opinion.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.850
But REST is about
these three things.

00:02:59.850 --> 00:03:01.830
We have resources.

00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:04.410
And those resources
are your things,

00:03:04.410 --> 00:03:07.300
the things that you care
about on your business logic.

00:03:07.300 --> 00:03:11.610
So for instance, you could
think about accounts, products--

00:03:14.470 --> 00:03:17.030
I was going to say sales,
but that is not the word.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:17.530
Whatever.

00:03:17.530 --> 00:03:20.440
You can find many different
things, products, accounts,

00:03:20.440 --> 00:03:21.160
or whatever.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.140
That is the main example.

00:03:23.140 --> 00:03:27.380
Then you have the methods, which
is what you can do with those.

00:03:27.380 --> 00:03:30.387
So you could create
a new customer.

00:03:30.387 --> 00:03:31.220
You could delete it.

00:03:31.220 --> 00:03:32.740
You could update a customer.

00:03:32.740 --> 00:03:35.230
You could do many of the
things that you can do.

00:03:35.230 --> 00:03:37.720
And the whole point
is that in a REST API

00:03:37.720 --> 00:03:40.030
those actions that you
can do are actually

00:03:40.030 --> 00:03:43.101
tied to well-understood
HTTP methods.

00:03:43.101 --> 00:03:43.600
Right?

00:03:43.600 --> 00:03:44.480
So you have POST.

00:03:44.480 --> 00:03:46.480
When you do POST, normally
you create a new one.

00:03:46.480 --> 00:03:50.170
When you do PUT, you give
a key, you update it.

00:03:50.170 --> 00:03:55.540
GET is to get stuff,
as it says, et cetera.

00:03:55.540 --> 00:03:57.850
And finally we have
the representation.

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:00.330
And representation, in
the case of rest the APIs,

00:04:00.330 --> 00:04:02.470
is normally JSON.

00:04:02.470 --> 00:04:09.340
So for example, we can have here
our /product that represents

00:04:09.340 --> 00:04:13.310
our resource, so all the
products that we have.

00:04:13.310 --> 00:04:17.000
If we do a POST and send this
JSON here, what we're doing

00:04:17.000 --> 00:04:22.780
is we're creating a new
resource with that POST method,

00:04:22.780 --> 00:04:24.080
pretty simple.

00:04:24.080 --> 00:04:26.950
Now, this is REST, right?

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:28.220
This is not REST.

00:04:28.220 --> 00:04:29.470
This is gRPC.

00:04:29.470 --> 00:04:31.630
But with gRPC you
can actually do

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:34.240
things that are very,
very, very similar to REST.

00:04:34.240 --> 00:04:37.390
You can create REST, not
RESTful, but RESTish--

00:04:37.390 --> 00:04:38.590
I decided to call them--

00:04:38.590 --> 00:04:41.860
RESTish APIs that basically
allow you to do the same things

00:04:41.860 --> 00:04:46.600
with the same style but
with a different way

00:04:46.600 --> 00:04:48.130
of naming the resources.

00:04:48.130 --> 00:04:50.380
Normally, in this case, it
could be product service.

00:04:50.380 --> 00:04:53.310
You can create them
calling it, create method.

00:04:53.310 --> 00:04:55.900
And the representation,
rather than being JSON,

00:04:55.900 --> 00:04:57.160
is protocol buffers.

00:04:57.160 --> 00:04:59.901
How many of you have used
protocol buffers before?

00:04:59.901 --> 00:05:00.400
OK.

00:05:00.400 --> 00:05:03.340
So just a quick description
of protocol buffers,

00:05:03.340 --> 00:05:05.740
protocol buffers is the
way we do everything

00:05:05.740 --> 00:05:07.330
at Google, basically.

00:05:07.330 --> 00:05:09.520
Many of the teams at Google,
we say that what we do

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:11.734
is we decode protocol
buffers, and then

00:05:11.734 --> 00:05:13.150
we code them again
somewhere else.

00:05:13.150 --> 00:05:15.370
That's pretty much what
you do most of the time.

00:05:15.370 --> 00:05:18.160
They're basically like JSON,
but when you encode them

00:05:18.160 --> 00:05:20.050
they're encoded
as binary format.

00:05:20.050 --> 00:05:22.530
Then coding and decoding
is way faster and also

00:05:22.530 --> 00:05:24.280
more performance when
you want to send it.

00:05:24.280 --> 00:05:26.680
At the same time, they
are better for versioning.

00:05:26.680 --> 00:05:29.860
So if you have never
used protocol buffers

00:05:29.860 --> 00:05:31.390
I think it's a good thing to do.

00:05:31.390 --> 00:05:32.860
But today we're
going to be talking

00:05:32.860 --> 00:05:34.750
about REST APIs-- so JSON.

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:38.360
Cool.

00:05:38.360 --> 00:05:40.460
So let's talk about
the demo that we're

00:05:40.460 --> 00:05:43.170
going to be using today.

00:05:43.170 --> 00:05:44.990
We're going to be using
