00:08:06.190 --> 00:08:08.524
If I remember
correctly-- there you

00:08:08.524 --> 00:08:10.690
go-- if you write something
that doesn't correspond,

00:08:10.690 --> 00:08:13.230
it's not good
JSON, it will fail.

00:08:13.230 --> 00:08:16.140
And also, if you try
to send something

00:08:16.140 --> 00:08:18.890
to a URL that doesn't
exist, you will get a 404.

00:08:18.890 --> 00:08:21.690
If you get a 404 that
is kind of an ugly one,

00:08:21.690 --> 00:08:22.920
it's just some text.

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:24.570
So if you're
serving an API, this

00:08:24.570 --> 00:08:26.400
is probably not
what you want to do.

00:08:26.400 --> 00:08:29.100
But we'll see how Endpoints
actually fixes that directly

00:08:29.100 --> 00:08:30.570
without you having
to do anything.

00:08:39.480 --> 00:08:41.980
Demoing with two computers
at the same time is not easy.

00:08:41.980 --> 00:08:48.081
[CHUCKLES]

00:08:48.081 --> 00:08:48.580
OK.

00:08:48.580 --> 00:08:51.900
So we have that demo, right?

00:08:51.900 --> 00:08:57.850
And the next question is, well,
I want to let people use it,

00:08:57.850 --> 00:08:59.310
to use it, right?

00:08:59.310 --> 00:09:01.920
How can they use it?

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:04.020
What is the message
they should send?

00:09:04.020 --> 00:09:05.970
What is the message
they should receive?

00:09:05.970 --> 00:09:08.850
What are the paths
that are accepted?

00:09:08.850 --> 00:09:11.100
What are the methods on those
paths that are accepted?

00:09:11.100 --> 00:09:11.790
Right?

00:09:11.790 --> 00:09:13.350
And you could do many things.

00:09:13.350 --> 00:09:15.170
You could just tell them.

00:09:15.170 --> 00:09:17.760
You can go and talk to every
single one of the developers

00:09:17.760 --> 00:09:20.790
and tell them, oh,
yeah, it's /echo.

00:09:20.790 --> 00:09:22.410
And it needs to be POST.

00:09:22.410 --> 00:09:24.740
It needs to send JSON
that has message--

00:09:24.740 --> 00:09:27.130
but message needs to be
lowercase, by the way,

00:09:27.130 --> 00:09:30.215
just in case you forget.

00:09:30.215 --> 00:09:31.590
It's something
that you could do,

00:09:31.590 --> 00:09:33.930
but I don't recommend
that, especially if you're

00:09:33.930 --> 00:09:35.190
serving this to the world.

00:09:39.020 --> 00:09:43.450
So what do we do instead?

00:09:43.450 --> 00:09:46.250
Well, we could show the code.

00:09:46.250 --> 00:09:49.960
And in this case, I could
say, right, I'm telling you.

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:51.520
I've showed you the code.

00:09:51.520 --> 00:09:54.880
So you can see this code.

00:09:54.880 --> 00:09:58.480
If you know how to write some
Go code, you'll be like, OK,

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:00.730
so there it is right there.

00:10:00.730 --> 00:10:03.490
If you send a POST
method to echo,

00:10:03.490 --> 00:10:05.770
then the echoHandler
function is cool.

00:10:05.770 --> 00:10:07.450
So then I could
follow up and try

00:10:07.450 --> 00:10:09.760
to understand what we're
trying to decode, what are we

00:10:09.760 --> 00:10:11.690
trying to encode?

00:10:11.690 --> 00:10:14.000
Not perfect, but this
is a little bit better

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:17.100
than just telling people.

00:10:17.100 --> 00:10:18.752
Or you could go contract first.

00:10:18.752 --> 00:10:20.460
And by contract first
what I'm saying is,

00:10:20.460 --> 00:10:22.680
rather than writing the
code and telling people

00:10:22.680 --> 00:10:25.110
about it, what I'm going
to do is I'm actually

00:10:25.110 --> 00:10:27.080
going to agree with people.

00:10:27.080 --> 00:10:28.050
This is my API.

00:10:28.050 --> 00:10:30.030
This is how it's
going to be used.

00:10:30.030 --> 00:10:33.869
And from there, I'm going
to start writing my code.

00:10:33.869 --> 00:10:35.410
And now this has a
bunch of benefits.

00:10:35.410 --> 00:10:39.060
One of the benefits it has is
that it improves collaboration

00:10:39.060 --> 00:10:41.280
across teams or across projects.

00:10:41.280 --> 00:10:45.547
If I clarify my API
clearly, afterwards I

00:10:45.547 --> 00:10:47.880
could change completely the
way I'm implementing behind.

00:10:47.880 --> 00:10:51.060
I could move from App
Engine to Compute Engine.

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:52.780
I could do whatever I want.

00:10:52.780 --> 00:10:54.990
If I don't break that
API, I should not

00:10:54.990 --> 00:10:57.660
be impacting any of the teams
that are using my service.

00:10:57.660 --> 00:10:59.370
So that is pretty important.

00:10:59.370 --> 00:11:02.490
But also, there is
many other ones.

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:03.990
And what are you
looking for when

00:11:03.990 --> 00:11:06.360
you're talking about the
way you document APIs?

00:11:06.360 --> 00:11:06.860
Right?

00:11:06.860 --> 00:11:08.730
Like, we're saying,
if you specify

00:11:08.730 --> 00:11:12.240
very well what you can do
with an API, then you're good.

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:13.621
You get all these advantages.

00:11:13.621 --> 00:11:15.120
Well, the first
thing you need to do

00:11:15.120 --> 00:11:16.980
is you need to be very accurate.

00:11:16.980 --> 00:11:19.740
You need to specifically
say all the things

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:23.940
that you need to do to use my
service, which means that also

00:11:23.940 --> 00:11:25.480
you need to be very expressive.

00:11:25.480 --> 00:11:27.480
Because imagine that if
the language that you're

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:31.950
trying to use to express all
the things about your API

00:11:31.950 --> 00:11:35.430
doesn't allow you to
say, oh, by the way,

00:11:35.430 --> 00:11:37.920
the JSON object
should look like this.

00:11:37.920 --> 00:11:39.649
And finally, it
needs to be readable.

00:11:39.649 --> 00:11:42.190
And when I say readable, there's
actually two readables here.

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:45.690
There's readable by humans
and readable by machines.

00:11:45.690 --> 00:11:46.200
Right?

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:48.600
I want something
that I, as a person,

00:11:48.600 --> 00:11:51.930
can go, and read, and understand
correctly, and have a very good

00:11:51.930 --> 00:11:53.940
idea of how to use this API.

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:56.100
But also, as a
software engineer--

00:11:56.100 --> 00:11:58.470
which is a kind
of person maybe--

00:11:58.470 --> 00:12:00.930
what I want to do
is I want to parse

00:12:00.930 --> 00:12:06.030
that description of the API so I
can build stuff on top of that.

00:12:06.030 --> 00:12:09.600
So free text, is it accurate?

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:10.370
No.

00:12:10.370 --> 00:12:13.590
Free text feels accurate
but is way too artsy.

00:12:13.590 --> 00:12:15.900
You can write many things
in many different ways that

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:18.500
might be ambiguous,
especially if you're not

00:12:18.500 --> 00:12:20.730
a native English
speaker, like me.

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:22.260
There's things that
you could write

00:12:22.260 --> 00:12:24.260
that actually means
something slightly different

00:12:24.260 --> 00:12:25.400
depending on the context.

00:12:25.400 --> 00:12:26.880
So free text, not the best.

00:12:26.880 --> 00:12:28.860
But expressiveness,
it's amazing.

00:12:28.860 --> 00:12:30.780
You can express
everything in the world

00:12:30.780 --> 00:12:33.880
with free text, so, of course.

00:12:33.880 --> 00:12:37.887
And readability, it is awesome
for humans, awful for machines.

00:12:37.887 --> 00:12:39.720
We're working on that
with machine learning.

00:12:39.720 --> 00:12:42.660
But I would not
recommend using machine

00:12:42.660 --> 00:12:45.210
learning to parse
recommendation for APIs

00:12:45.210 --> 00:12:46.410
to create all the tools.

00:12:46.410 --> 00:12:48.270
That-- it's cool, as a demo.

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:50.890
But I don't think
it's a very good idea.

00:12:50.890 --> 00:12:52.450
So let's go down the other side.

00:12:52.450 --> 00:12:54.320
It's incredibly accurate.

00:12:54.320 --> 00:12:56.040
It says exactly what
you want to say.

00:12:56.040 --> 00:12:57.874
There's no ambiguity.

00:12:57.874 --> 00:12:59.790
It is very expressive,
because you're actually

00:12:59.790 --> 00:13:01.260
writing the whole domain.

00:13:01.260 --> 00:13:03.240
Very often the
source code does--

00:13:03.240 --> 00:13:05.245
expresses way more
than just your API.

00:13:05.245 --> 00:13:07.910
It expresses exactly
all the process.

00:13:07.910 --> 00:13:11.430
But readability-wise, I'd say
that even though for machines

00:13:11.430 --> 00:13:12.826
it's pretty easy to read--

00:13:12.826 --> 00:13:14.700
I mean, it depends on
the prominent language,

00:13:14.700 --> 00:13:16.560
but most of the
prominent languages

00:13:16.560 --> 00:13:18.810
are done to be parsed.

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:21.660
Breathability for human
beings is not that good.

00:13:21.660 --> 00:13:25.170
Because first, you need to
talk to someone that programs

00:13:25.170 --> 00:13:27.000
and in your
programming language.

00:13:27.000 --> 00:13:28.685
I program in Go.

00:13:28.685 --> 00:13:30.810
If you're a programmer that
has never written in Go

00:13:30.810 --> 00:13:31.770
and only knows--

00:13:31.770 --> 00:13:34.170
I don't know,
let's say, Haskell.

00:13:34.170 --> 00:13:36.900
From Haskell to Go,
there's a big difference.

00:13:36.900 --> 00:13:39.900
If you need to understand
Go to be able to use my API,

00:13:39.900 --> 00:13:42.610
that's not a good thing.

00:13:42.610 --> 00:13:47.470
And then finally we
have design languages.

00:13:47.470 --> 00:13:49.607
And those design languages,
there's many of them.

00:13:49.607 --> 00:13:51.190
But the design
language is-- basically

00:13:51.190 --> 00:13:53.790
the idea is that you create
a new language in between.

00:13:53.790 --> 00:13:54.580
Right?

00:13:54.580 --> 00:13:56.980
That language, the
whole idea is that it's

00:13:56.980 --> 00:13:59.230
accurate and
expressive enough to be

00:13:59.230 --> 00:14:03.580
able to describe your API
without ambiguity but not

00:14:03.580 --> 00:14:04.690
completely.

00:14:04.690 --> 00:14:06.970
You're not going to be able
to express the whole thing

00:14:06.970 --> 00:14:08.800
that your API might
do in all the corner

00:14:08.800 --> 00:14:12.100
cases with design language.

00:14:12.100 --> 00:14:13.570
It's not a programming language.

00:14:13.570 --> 00:14:16.520
But on the other hand,
it's more readable.

00:14:16.520 --> 00:14:18.910
It is more readable, in
general, for human beings.

00:14:18.910 --> 00:14:21.160
And it's definitely more
readable for machines.

00:14:21.160 --> 00:14:22.250
That is the whole point.

00:14:22.250 --> 00:14:25.960
You're building something
that is very possible.

00:14:25.960 --> 00:14:28.570
So it feels like we should
go with the one that

00:14:28.570 --> 00:14:30.310
has the better tradeoffs.

00:14:30.310 --> 00:14:32.910
So I'd go with the last one.

00:14:32.910 --> 00:14:35.070
Which one?

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:37.320
Well, you could go with RAML.

00:14:37.320 --> 00:14:39.200
You could go with WSDL--

00:14:39.200 --> 00:14:41.170
I think it's pronounced.

00:14:41.170 --> 00:14:44.940
You can go with WADL, or WADL,
depending on where you're from.

00:14:44.940 --> 00:14:49.200
Or you can go with OPEN API
Initiative also previously

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:51.390
known as Swagger.

00:14:51.390 --> 00:14:54.231
How many of you
have used Swagger?

00:14:54.231 --> 00:14:54.730
Cool.

00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:55.230
OK.

00:14:55.230 --> 00:14:59.120
So that is the one we're using.

00:14:59.120 --> 00:15:00.580
If you use Swagger,
the whole point

00:15:00.580 --> 00:15:04.000
is that you're able to
do many cool things.

00:15:04.000 --> 00:15:06.940
You specify mainly
for components.

00:15:06.940 --> 00:15:09.700
So when you're
doing a Swagger API,

00:15:09.700 --> 00:15:12.430
you have information
about the API itself.

00:15:12.430 --> 00:15:14.520
It's basically documentation.

00:15:14.520 --> 00:15:15.350
You have paths.

00:15:15.350 --> 00:15:20.817
So what are the paths in
the URL that you're serving?

00:15:20.817 --> 00:15:22.150
What are the operations in them?

00:15:22.150 --> 00:15:24.520
What are the methods for
every single one of the paths?

00:15:24.520 --> 00:15:27.490
And then for input
and output, you're

00:15:27.490 --> 00:15:29.050
basically defining
those messages.

00:15:29.050 --> 00:15:31.610
Those messages are what
we call definitions.

00:15:31.610 --> 00:15:34.600
And finally, there are
security definitions.

00:15:34.600 --> 00:15:39.520
So basically, what you're going
to have is for information,

00:15:39.520 --> 00:15:41.730
in this case, we have
description, "A Google Cloud

00:15:41.730 --> 00:15:45.460
Endpoints API example," a
title, a version, a host--

00:15:45.460 --> 00:15:49.210
which is, where should
I go to find this API?

00:15:49.210 --> 00:15:52.175
And then you're going to have
also, what are you receiving?

00:15:52.175 --> 00:15:53.050
What are you sending?

00:15:53.050 --> 00:15:54.530
So in this case,
application JSON.

00:15:54.530 --> 00:15:57.010
We're receiving JSON,
sending back JSON.

00:15:57.010 --> 00:15:59.275
And we're serving
everything on HTTPS.

00:15:59.275 --> 00:16:02.110
All of this doesn't
say anything about what

00:16:02.110 --> 00:16:03.280
you can do with the API.

00:16:03.280 --> 00:16:04.750
This is just like
the documentation

00:16:04.750 --> 00:16:09.570
for humans to read and a
little bit of where to find it.

00:16:13.660 --> 00:16:15.650
I went too fast with that one.

00:16:15.650 --> 00:16:16.909
Then we have the paths.

00:16:16.909 --> 00:16:19.450
And in paths, for instance, in
this case you could say, well,

00:16:19.450 --> 00:16:23.360
if you go to /echo and
you send a request,

00:16:23.360 --> 00:16:27.210
which is a POST method, then
you have this operation.

00:16:27.210 --> 00:16:27.850
OK?

00:16:27.850 --> 00:16:29.890
That operation, what
you're doing is saying,

00:16:29.890 --> 00:16:31.800
well, there's the description.

00:16:31.800 --> 00:16:32.980
This is the operationID.

00:16:32.980 --> 00:16:35.380
And we're going to see
what this is useful for.

00:16:35.380 --> 00:16:37.720
OperationID is not compulsory,
but it's very useful

00:16:37.720 --> 00:16:39.820
when you're generating code.

00:16:39.820 --> 00:16:44.170
And you'll again say what it's
producing, application JSON.

00:16:44.170 --> 00:16:46.352
And then you have
responses and parameters.

00:16:46.352 --> 00:16:48.310
And this is very interesting,
because basically

00:16:48.310 --> 00:16:53.530
what you're saying is-- what
do I get from the request?

00:16:53.530 --> 00:16:55.630
So what are the parameters
that I'm receiving?

00:16:55.630 --> 00:16:59.030
And what do I send back?

00:16:59.030 --> 00:17:01.200
In our case, the responses
we're going to say,

00:17:01.200 --> 00:17:02.690
well, there's two cases.

00:17:02.690 --> 00:17:07.760
One is 200, also known
as the status code, OK.

00:17:07.760 --> 00:17:11.000
And in which case, we're
sending the echo back.

00:17:11.000 --> 00:17:12.169
Our [AUDIO OUT] says.

00:17:12.169 --> 00:17:13.460
It just sends the message back.

00:17:13.460 --> 00:17:14.660
So that's what it does.

00:17:14.660 --> 00:17:18.980
And it sends something
with schema, echoMessage.

00:17:18.980 --> 00:17:21.051
And that is actually a
reference to a definition

00:17:21.051 --> 00:17:22.550
that we're going
to see in a minute.

00:17:22.550 --> 00:17:25.790
So basically you're saying,
what are you going to send?

00:17:25.790 --> 00:17:28.339
But in other cases,
default, we're

00:17:28.339 --> 00:17:30.030
going to be sending
an error message.

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:32.090
And the error message
is also well-defined.

00:17:32.090 --> 00:17:33.923
So we're saying it's
an error message, which

00:17:33.923 --> 00:17:37.640
is also in definitions.

00:17:37.640 --> 00:17:38.740
What do we receive?

00:17:38.740 --> 00:17:40.810
Well, that you're going
to have in parameters.

00:17:40.810 --> 00:17:44.900
And we're going to say that
we're receiving a message.

00:17:44.900 --> 00:17:48.850
So the schema is
again the echoMessage.

00:17:48.850 --> 00:17:51.070
But what is interesting
is we are actually saying

00:17:51.070 --> 00:17:53.140
these will be in the body.

00:17:53.140 --> 00:17:53.669
Right?

00:17:53.669 --> 00:17:55.210
You could have many
different places.

00:17:55.210 --> 00:17:57.197
You could have this as
a parameter in the URL.

00:17:57.197 --> 00:17:58.780
You could have it
as part of the path.

00:17:58.780 --> 00:18:01.150
There is many places where
that information could be.

00:18:01.150 --> 00:18:04.510
And Swagger, Open
API specification

00:18:04.510 --> 00:18:08.830
allows you to specify exactly
where this is coming from.

00:18:12.000 --> 00:18:13.810
What does the
definition look like?

00:18:13.810 --> 00:18:15.345
Well, error message.

00:18:15.345 --> 00:18:17.470
We're saying, for instance,
this is a type: object.

00:18:17.470 --> 00:18:19.450
So it has a bunch of fields.

00:18:19.450 --> 00:18:22.960
And the fields are both
required, messaging code,

00:18:22.960 --> 00:18:24.850
and messages of type: string.

00:18:24.850 --> 00:18:26.200
Code is an integer.

00:18:26.200 --> 00:18:28.390
And you can even say
it's from 100 to 600,

00:18:28.390 --> 00:18:30.700
because that's what
HTTP codes are for.

00:18:33.110 --> 00:18:33.610
Cool.

00:18:33.610 --> 00:18:35.380
So what do I get from this?

00:18:35.380 --> 00:18:36.900
Actually, a bunch of stuff.

00:18:36.900 --> 00:18:39.400
You get really cool things.

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:41.400
So you can create
documentation from this that

00:18:41.400 --> 00:18:43.290
is very readable for humans.

00:18:43.290 --> 00:18:45.030
You can create code.

00:18:45.030 --> 00:18:47.230
And you can even create
developer portals,

00:18:47.230 --> 00:18:48.960
which is very useful.

00:18:48.960 --> 00:18:53.040
If you've ever used
any Google API, for me,

00:18:53.040 --> 00:18:55.500
one of the coolest things
we have is the Google--

00:18:55.500 --> 00:18:57.870
the API playground,
the API Explorer.

00:18:57.870 --> 00:19:00.039
Where you can go,
search your APIs,

00:19:00.039 --> 00:19:01.830
go send the message
directly without having

00:19:01.830 --> 00:19:03.180
to install anything.

00:19:03.180 --> 00:19:06.810
So you understand the API before
you start writing any code.

00:19:06.810 --> 00:19:08.430
So let's demo that.

00:19:16.221 --> 00:19:16.720
OK.

00:19:16.720 --> 00:19:18.784
So what I'm going to
do first is make sure

00:19:18.784 --> 00:19:20.200
that my server is
running locally.

00:19:22.980 --> 00:19:29.690
Not that one but this one here.

00:19:29.690 --> 00:19:30.260
OK.

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:32.332
There it is.

00:19:32.332 --> 00:19:33.790
I should get
something complaining.

00:19:33.790 --> 00:19:35.430
There you go.

00:19:35.430 --> 00:19:35.930
OK.

00:19:35.930 --> 00:19:37.870
So my server is now running.

00:19:37.870 --> 00:19:46.830
And what I can do is
I can visit editor--

00:19:46.830 --> 00:19:53.139
wrong window-- editor,
Swagger editor.

00:19:53.139 --> 00:19:53.680
Wrong window.

00:19:53.680 --> 00:19:55.770
I have too many windows.

00:19:55.770 --> 00:19:58.080
There you go, this one.

00:19:58.080 --> 00:20:00.030
And with this one,
what I'm doing

00:20:00.030 --> 00:20:03.570
is basically I just copied
that Swagger definition

00:20:03.570 --> 00:20:04.910
onto this editor.

00:20:04.910 --> 00:20:06.840
This editor is something
that is very simple.

00:20:06.840 --> 00:20:07.340
It's online.

00:20:07.340 --> 00:20:09.014
You don't need to
install anything.

00:20:09.014 --> 00:20:10.680
Now the cool thing
about this is that we

00:20:10.680 --> 00:20:14.850
can see on the right side
this documentation, where

00:20:14.850 --> 00:20:17.610
you can see if you
send POST to echo,

00:20:17.610 --> 00:20:19.710
the description, you
have all the parameters,

00:20:19.710 --> 00:20:22.300
you can understand quite
easily how to use it.

00:20:22.300 --> 00:20:24.780
The more documentation you
add in the specification file,

00:20:24.780 --> 00:20:26.670
you get a documentation
that is easier

00:20:26.670 --> 00:20:29.220
to use for your developers.

00:20:29.220 --> 00:20:32.979
And then on top of that,
you can try an operation.

00:20:32.979 --> 00:20:34.770
You can say exactly
what you're going to be

00:20:34.770 --> 00:20:37.500
sending, what you're receiving.

00:20:37.500 --> 00:20:43.470
It is able to tell
you-- actually,

00:20:43.470 --> 00:20:47.190
the echoMessage object has
a field, which is required,

00:20:47.190 --> 00:20:48.820
message, and you then send it.

00:20:48.820 --> 00:20:50.970
So it's able to do
validation for yourself.

00:20:50.970 --> 00:20:54.600
It's able to do this
validation even in a proxy

00:20:54.600 --> 00:20:57.840
that you don't need to write
yourself, which is pretty cool.

00:20:57.840 --> 00:21:05.410
So you can write, hello, Google
Cloud Next, send it there.

00:21:05.410 --> 00:21:09.430
And we get a response with
a message coming back.

00:21:09.430 --> 00:21:12.590
And we can see what
it looks exactly.

00:21:12.590 --> 00:21:15.320
And if you think
about this, if you're

00:21:15.320 --> 00:21:19.160
offering these tools
to the developers that

00:21:19.160 --> 00:21:21.380
are trying to use
your API, this is

00:21:21.380 --> 00:21:24.800
way more useful than
amazing documentation.

00:21:24.800 --> 00:21:27.680
In my opinion, documentation
is great to read it,

00:21:27.680 --> 00:21:31.170
but when I want to use
your API, I want this.

00:21:31.170 --> 00:21:34.010
I want to be able to find
what operation is doing what

00:21:34.010 --> 00:21:35.510
and what are the
exact fields that I

00:21:35.510 --> 00:21:37.381
need to send, try
it, and then write

00:21:37.381 --> 00:21:38.630
my code that will do the same.

00:21:50.660 --> 00:21:53.190
There's an important thing if
you try to do this at home.

00:21:53.190 --> 00:21:57.100
There is the fact that there is
what is called the Cross-Origin

00:21:57.100 --> 00:21:58.750
HTTP request.

00:21:58.750 --> 00:22:01.870
I don't know if you heard
about this, CORS, C-O-R-S.

00:22:01.870 --> 00:22:03.400
The whole point is security.

00:22:03.400 --> 00:22:06.610
If you try to do this
onto a server that is not

00:22:06.610 --> 00:22:08.350
running locally,
you start to need

00:22:08.350 --> 00:22:10.210
to take care of these things.

00:22:10.210 --> 00:22:12.640
There's documentation
about that.

00:22:12.640 --> 00:22:15.550
I feel like every single person
that has tried to use this tool

00:22:15.550 --> 00:22:18.770
has discovered the
options method.

00:22:18.770 --> 00:22:21.520
It's a method in
HTTP that nobody

00:22:21.520 --> 00:22:24.040
uses except for this tool.

00:22:24.040 --> 00:22:26.980
So you need to support it.

00:22:26.980 --> 00:22:27.880
Cool.

00:22:27.880 --> 00:22:28.780
OK.

00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:32.200
So this brings a
lot of value, right?

00:22:32.200 --> 00:22:34.450
But again, this is still
not Cloud Endpoints.

00:22:34.450 --> 00:22:35.600
What is Cloud Endpoints?

00:22:35.600 --> 00:22:37.240
Well, Cloud Endpoints
is something

00:22:37.240 --> 00:22:40.110
that has been built to--

00:22:40.110 --> 00:22:43.140
given just this specification--

00:22:43.140 --> 00:22:45.510
give you more value, give
you a bunch of things

00:22:45.510 --> 00:22:46.830
that you need to do.

00:22:46.830 --> 00:22:49.450
If you want to expose
your API to the world,

00:22:49.450 --> 00:22:52.110
there's things like
monitoring authentication

00:22:52.110 --> 00:22:53.820
that you should be doing.

00:22:53.820 --> 00:22:56.290
But you do not need
to write those.

00:22:56.290 --> 00:22:58.666
So that is exactly what
Cloud Endpoints does.

00:22:58.666 --> 00:22:59.290
Let's see that.

00:23:02.694 --> 00:23:03.194
Whoops.

00:23:07.600 --> 00:23:09.490
OK.

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:12.190
So if you've used Cloud--

00:23:12.190 --> 00:23:16.671
has anyone used Cloud Endpoints
previously, like one year ago?

00:23:16.671 --> 00:23:17.170
OK.

00:23:17.170 --> 00:23:19.330
So these may be familiar to you.

00:23:19.330 --> 00:23:21.250
Cloud Endpoints is
a framework that

00:23:21.250 --> 00:23:24.880
runs on App Engine
standard environment,

00:23:24.880 --> 00:23:29.470
either for Java or
Python, and that's it.

00:23:29.470 --> 00:23:30.700
No.

00:23:30.700 --> 00:23:31.422
Not anymore.

00:23:31.422 --> 00:23:33.130
That's the whole point
of Cloud Endpoints

00:23:33.130 --> 00:23:35.980
is getting that idea that
people loved and make

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:39.220
it accessible to everywhere--
no matter if you're

00:23:39.220 --> 00:23:42.700
running Java, Python,
or Go, or PHP,

00:23:42.700 --> 00:23:44.050
or whatever language you want.

00:23:44.050 --> 00:23:47.260
And if you're running it on
APP Engine, or in a Container,

00:23:47.260 --> 00:23:50.437
or somewhere in a machine
that is hiding in your closet.

00:23:50.437 --> 00:23:51.520
We do not care about that.

00:23:51.520 --> 00:23:53.020
You are able to run it wherever.

00:23:56.230 --> 00:24:01.427
So what it is is an API
gateway, and it's an API gateway

00:24:01.427 --> 00:24:02.260
that is distributed.

00:24:02.260 --> 00:24:06.100
We're going to see that later
on the architecture diagrams.

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:09.100
But the cool thing is that
this is very integrated, right?

00:24:09.100 --> 00:24:12.970
So it is integrated with
deployments for App Engine.

00:24:12.970 --> 00:24:15.160
If you're using App Engine
flexible environment,

00:24:15.160 --> 00:24:16.700
it's a great experience.

00:24:16.700 --> 00:24:18.670
It is simply-- you are just--

00:24:18.670 --> 00:24:20.890
one little field in
your app in the demo.

00:24:20.890 --> 00:24:22.300
You redeploy and that's it.

00:24:22.300 --> 00:24:24.020
You're done, which is amazing.

00:24:24.020 --> 00:24:25.840
But for Container
Engine it is-- which

00:24:25.840 --> 00:24:28.040
is what we're going to be
doing-- it's very easy.

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:30.686
And if you want to do
anything else, it is doable.

00:24:30.686 --> 00:24:32.560
It might be a little
bit harder to implement,

00:24:32.560 --> 00:24:34.360
but it is definitely doable.

00:24:34.360 --> 00:24:36.730
And it also integrates
with Stackdriver logging

00:24:36.730 --> 00:24:38.080
and Stackdriver Trace.

00:24:38.080 --> 00:24:43.630
So whenever you
receive a request,

00:24:43.630 --> 00:24:46.180
you're going to be able
to see that in your logs

00:24:46.180 --> 00:24:47.230
and also in traces.

00:24:47.230 --> 00:24:50.140
So you're going to be able
to track, to measure latency,

00:24:50.140 --> 00:24:51.640
and things like
that, without having

00:24:51.640 --> 00:24:54.670
to carry it in your code.

00:24:54.670 --> 00:24:58.160
So the key features
are authentication.

00:24:58.160 --> 00:25:01.120
So you're able to authenticate
users and control who

00:25:01.120 --> 00:25:03.190
has access to what.

00:25:03.190 --> 00:25:07.300
All of this, just
by adding things,

00:25:07.300 --> 00:25:11.230
security definitions to
your open API specification.

00:25:11.230 --> 00:25:14.330
You're going to get also
logging and monitoring.

00:25:14.330 --> 00:25:16.680
And this is really fast.

00:25:16.680 --> 00:25:17.800
It is incredibly fast.

00:25:17.800 --> 00:25:19.550
And it scales really well.

00:25:19.550 --> 00:25:21.110
And we're going
to see why it's--

00:25:21.110 --> 00:25:22.710
I'm not a marketing
person, so I'm

00:25:22.710 --> 00:25:27.620
going to explain why this is
fast and scales very well.

00:25:27.620 --> 00:25:29.290
You can run it anywhere.

00:25:29.290 --> 00:25:33.730
You can run the Endpoints
thing, that API gateway,

00:25:33.730 --> 00:25:35.860
you can run it on App Engine.

00:25:35.860 --> 00:25:37.360
So if you run on
App Engine, you can

00:25:37.360 --> 00:25:39.910
run on App Engine flexible
for whatever you want to do,

00:25:39.910 --> 00:25:43.420
or App Engine standard with the
frameworks for Java and Python.

00:25:43.420 --> 00:25:45.010
You can run it on
Container Engine.

00:25:45.010 --> 00:25:46.870
You can run it on
Compute Engine.

00:25:46.870 --> 00:25:48.400
Or really, you can
run it anywhere

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:50.530
where you are able
to run a Container.

00:25:50.530 --> 00:25:54.580
If it runs on a
Container, you can run it.

00:25:54.580 --> 00:25:56.560
So how does this actually work?

00:25:59.004 --> 00:26:00.170
