00:26:08.440 --> 00:26:13.780
So first what you do
is you deploy an API.

00:26:13.780 --> 00:26:15.700
What you do is you
code with GCloud.

00:26:15.700 --> 00:26:18.080
You send the API specification,
that openapi.yaml

00:26:18.080 --> 00:26:21.730
file that we saw before, and you
send it to the Google Service

00:26:21.730 --> 00:26:22.600
Management.

00:26:22.600 --> 00:26:25.300
What you're saying here is
you're declaring something new.

00:26:25.300 --> 00:26:28.960
And you're going to get
an API name and a version.

00:26:28.960 --> 00:26:32.757
Then when you run that
gateway, the gateway

00:26:32.757 --> 00:26:35.215
is going to communicate with
the Google Service Management.

00:26:35.215 --> 00:26:37.220
And it's going to say,
hey, I want to serve

00:26:37.220 --> 00:26:40.000
this API name and this version.

00:26:40.000 --> 00:26:41.252
Give me everything I need.

00:26:41.252 --> 00:26:43.210
Basically it's going to
get all the information

00:26:43.210 --> 00:26:46.030
from the API, plus
authentication, and all

00:26:46.030 --> 00:26:47.350
the stuff.

00:26:47.350 --> 00:26:49.710
This is just the
deployment part.

00:26:49.710 --> 00:26:51.040
That's it.

00:26:51.040 --> 00:26:54.570
So that's why you can run that
Container Engine anywhere,

00:26:54.570 --> 00:26:56.150
as long as you can--

00:26:56.150 --> 00:26:58.800
Container Engine, sorry,
that container for Endpoints.

00:26:58.800 --> 00:27:01.620
As long as you're able to use
the Google Service Management

00:27:01.620 --> 00:27:06.560
API, which is simply a REST
API, you're able to do this.

00:27:06.560 --> 00:27:09.530
And then when you get
a request, no matter

00:27:09.530 --> 00:27:12.650
what client is sending it,
rather than going directly

00:27:12.650 --> 00:27:16.750
to your code, it goes
first to that container.

00:27:16.750 --> 00:27:19.040
The container checks with
the Google Service Control

00:27:19.040 --> 00:27:22.260
if this is allowed or not.

00:27:22.260 --> 00:27:27.270
If it is allowed, it sends
it to your actual code.

00:27:27.270 --> 00:27:29.980
That, again, could
be running anywhere.

00:27:29.980 --> 00:27:32.580
And finally it logs everything
to Stackdriver for logs,

00:27:32.580 --> 00:27:35.490
and monitoring, and traces.

00:27:35.490 --> 00:27:36.310
That's it.

00:27:36.310 --> 00:27:38.110
This is the whole thing.

00:27:38.110 --> 00:27:40.757
Now, the important
thing is that--

00:27:40.757 --> 00:27:42.340
I don't know if
you're able to see it.

00:27:42.340 --> 00:27:43.900
I'm not really able to see it--

00:27:43.900 --> 00:27:47.530
but there's a little blue box
running both the Extensible

00:27:47.530 --> 00:27:50.230
Service Container and my code.

00:27:50.230 --> 00:27:52.000
That box is a node.

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:54.470
And I'm calling it a node
because it could be anything.

00:27:54.470 --> 00:27:56.440
It could be a path
on kubernetes,

00:27:56.440 --> 00:27:58.174
but it could also
be an instance,

00:27:58.174 --> 00:27:59.590
an actual node, a
virtual machine,

00:27:59.590 --> 00:28:00.798
or it could be anything else.

00:28:00.798 --> 00:28:04.420
The whole point is that this
is running together, right?

00:28:04.420 --> 00:28:05.890
And the point of
running together

00:28:05.890 --> 00:28:09.170
is that since you're
exactly on the same machine,

00:28:09.170 --> 00:28:11.950
there's no network
hubs to be added.

00:28:11.950 --> 00:28:15.170
So this is very fast.

00:28:15.170 --> 00:28:19.260
So it is faster and
scales with your app.

00:28:19.260 --> 00:28:21.120
What do I mean by it
scales with your app?

00:28:21.120 --> 00:28:26.210
You have one gateway per
container, or per unit

00:28:26.210 --> 00:28:31.945
of whatever you're trying
to use as your back-end.

00:28:31.945 --> 00:28:34.570
So you could put a
load balancer there,

00:28:34.570 --> 00:28:37.300
and the request will go
to the load balancer.

00:28:37.300 --> 00:28:39.760
And rather than going directly
from the load balancer

00:28:39.760 --> 00:28:44.770
to your code, you're sending
from the load balancer

00:28:44.770 --> 00:28:46.900
to the Extensible Service Proxy.

00:28:46.900 --> 00:28:50.770
And that one will call
the functions, the codes,

00:28:50.770 --> 00:28:53.356
as we said before, and then
send it to your back-end.

00:28:53.356 --> 00:28:57.260
And this you can
add more and more.

00:28:57.260 --> 00:29:01.810
As you can see here, the
Extensible Service Proxy SV

00:29:01.810 --> 00:29:06.640
is never the bottleneck, because
it keeps on scaling with you.

00:29:06.640 --> 00:29:08.950
So you can imagine how
this scales very well

00:29:08.950 --> 00:29:09.790
with kubernetes.

00:29:09.790 --> 00:29:12.050
You just create a pod
with two containers.

00:29:12.050 --> 00:29:13.970
