Now I have monitoring.

00:37:46.895 --> 00:37:50.300
I am able to see what's
going on with that measure.

00:37:50.300 --> 00:37:53.270
I'm able to start
to create alerts.

00:37:53.270 --> 00:37:56.450
I'm able to say-- normally,
I have around one request

00:37:56.450 --> 00:37:57.435
per second.

00:37:57.435 --> 00:37:59.810
If I don't have any requests
per second for a little bit,

00:37:59.810 --> 00:38:00.950
something's wrong.

00:38:00.950 --> 00:38:02.450
You're able to alert
your developers

00:38:02.450 --> 00:38:04.040
about these kind
of things, which

00:38:04.040 --> 00:38:07.790
is pretty amazing because
it's simply Stackdriver.

00:38:07.790 --> 00:38:12.470
You can also go here and see
a little bit more information

00:38:12.470 --> 00:38:13.340
about the logs.

00:38:13.340 --> 00:38:16.170
What are the requests
you actually received?

00:38:16.170 --> 00:38:19.000
And you can see there's
all this information.

00:38:19.000 --> 00:38:20.170
There's not much.

00:38:20.170 --> 00:38:22.840
But you can actually see
that for every single log

00:38:22.840 --> 00:38:25.660
what is the status
code that was returned

00:38:25.660 --> 00:38:27.558
and who rejected it, basically.

00:38:34.110 --> 00:38:34.790
OK.

00:38:34.790 --> 00:38:38.670
So as we said before,
this is amazing.

00:38:38.670 --> 00:38:41.380
Now everyone can use my service.

00:38:41.380 --> 00:38:43.210
But that's maybe not
what I want to do.

00:38:43.210 --> 00:38:45.490
I don't want everyone,
everyone in the world,

00:38:45.490 --> 00:38:47.320
to be able to use my service.

00:38:47.320 --> 00:38:50.260
For an echo server I'm
pretty confident we're fine.

00:38:50.260 --> 00:38:53.140
But if you're doing something
that involves actually

00:38:53.140 --> 00:38:55.310
requests that are
expensive, for instance,

00:38:55.310 --> 00:38:57.130
you might want to secure this.

00:38:57.130 --> 00:38:58.750
So how do we add authentication?

00:38:58.750 --> 00:39:02.670
We have monitoring, tracing,
how do we add authentication?

00:39:02.670 --> 00:39:05.910
Let's go back there.

00:39:05.910 --> 00:39:08.410
Let's talk about
authentication now.

00:39:08.410 --> 00:39:12.620
There's four methods that are
supported by Cloud Endpoints.

00:39:12.620 --> 00:39:16.750
There's Firebase,
Auth0, there's API keys,

00:39:16.750 --> 00:39:19.330
and there's JSON Web Tokens--

00:39:19.330 --> 00:39:24.680
also known as JWT, I've
heard, which is crazy.

00:39:24.680 --> 00:39:26.500
So what I'm going
to be demoing today

00:39:26.500 --> 00:39:30.460
is API keys only, because I
think it's pretty interesting.

00:39:30.460 --> 00:39:33.490
If you're a developer
and you have

00:39:33.490 --> 00:39:36.910
an API that you're exposing,
think about how hard would it

00:39:36.910 --> 00:39:39.160
be to implement this safely.

00:39:39.160 --> 00:39:42.190
To implement something
that anyone in the world

00:39:42.190 --> 00:39:46.720
can say, create a new API
key, just for themselves,

00:39:46.720 --> 00:39:49.210
and start using it, and make
sure that no one else has

00:39:49.210 --> 00:39:51.920
access to it, no one else--

00:39:51.920 --> 00:39:54.060
and that you're able to
successfully identify

00:39:54.060 --> 00:39:56.980
who is using what.

00:39:56.980 --> 00:40:00.100
So this is actually incredibly
simple on the Swagger

00:40:00.100 --> 00:40:00.780
point of view.

00:40:00.780 --> 00:40:02.530
In Swagger, everything
you're going to say

00:40:02.530 --> 00:40:04.905
is-- you're going to create
that security definition that

00:40:04.905 --> 00:40:06.280
says, API key.

00:40:06.280 --> 00:40:09.100
And you say, the
type is API key,

00:40:09.100 --> 00:40:13.780
and it's the parameter that's
going to be in the query.

00:40:13.780 --> 00:40:19.450
So basically, question mark
key equals that API key.

00:40:19.450 --> 00:40:21.220
That's what you're
specifying there.

00:40:21.220 --> 00:40:25.540
And then you're saying that
the POST method on echo

00:40:25.540 --> 00:40:28.210
is protected by API key.

00:40:28.210 --> 00:40:28.817
That's it.

00:40:28.817 --> 00:40:31.612
That's everything
you need to do.

00:40:31.612 --> 00:40:32.320
So let's do that.

00:40:38.880 --> 00:40:44.403
So here I should
have my openapi.yaml.

00:40:44.403 --> 00:40:47.000
Oops, 02.

00:40:47.000 --> 00:40:48.600
Openapi.yaml.

00:40:48.600 --> 00:40:50.990
Here on openapi.yaml it
just added these lines

00:40:50.990 --> 00:40:52.620
at the end, security
definitions saying

00:40:52.620 --> 00:40:56.510
there's an API key
that is in the query.

00:40:56.510 --> 00:40:59.930
And then I said that
I'm requiring API keys

00:40:59.930 --> 00:41:03.280
for POST on echo.

00:41:03.280 --> 00:41:05.820
So I'm going to do gcloud
service-management deploy

00:41:05.820 --> 00:41:07.534
openapi.yaml.

00:41:07.534 --> 00:41:08.950
And this is what
it's going to do.

00:41:08.950 --> 00:41:11.680
It's going to redeploy
it again, same thing.

00:41:11.680 --> 00:41:13.930
And now does that
mean that I'm actually

00:41:13.930 --> 00:41:17.110
squashing the previous
version and all of a sudden I

00:41:17.110 --> 00:41:20.901
just migrated everyone
to the new version?

00:41:20.901 --> 00:41:21.400
No.

00:41:21.400 --> 00:41:22.559
That'd be pretty awful.

00:41:22.559 --> 00:41:24.100
Because it would
mean that you're not

00:41:24.100 --> 00:41:26.080
able to do any tests, basically.

00:41:26.080 --> 00:41:29.090
What is going to happen is it's
going to give us a new version.

00:41:29.090 --> 00:41:31.220
And that version, no
one is using it yet.

00:41:31.220 --> 00:41:33.040
We're going to
need to then update

00:41:33.040 --> 00:41:36.430
our kubernetes
cluster, or App Engine,

00:41:36.430 --> 00:41:38.830
or whatever you're using
to use that new version.

00:41:38.830 --> 00:41:41.461
And then there you can do
rolling updates or whatever

00:41:41.461 --> 00:41:43.960
you'd normally do to make sure
that your new version doesn't

00:41:43.960 --> 00:41:46.510
break prod.

00:41:46.510 --> 00:41:51.520
So we've got our new
version right here.

00:41:51.520 --> 00:41:58.630
So I'm going to change my
container yaml, go to v--

00:41:58.630 --> 00:42:01.820
I forgot this one here.

00:42:05.820 --> 00:42:07.380
Whoa.

00:42:07.380 --> 00:42:10.830
Vim is fun sometimes.

00:42:10.830 --> 00:42:11.520
Cool.

00:42:11.520 --> 00:42:12.330
That looks good.

00:42:12.330 --> 00:42:12.829
OK.

00:42:12.829 --> 00:42:14.280
So I just changed that.

00:42:14.280 --> 00:42:18.260
And then kubectl apply.

00:42:18.260 --> 00:42:20.120
Now this changed the service.

00:42:20.120 --> 00:42:26.710
Now the new ESP container is
actually using the new version.

00:42:26.710 --> 00:42:35.430
So if we go back here
and try to send a message

00:42:35.430 --> 00:42:38.350
that should not work.

00:42:38.350 --> 00:42:39.160
That's it.

00:42:39.160 --> 00:42:44.140
Now it's-- we have API security,
API keys that is exposed here.

00:42:44.140 --> 00:42:46.300
Method doesn't allow
unregistered callers.

00:42:46.300 --> 00:42:49.630
Please use API key or the
form of API consumer identity

00:42:49.630 --> 00:42:51.040
to call this API.

00:42:51.040 --> 00:42:51.820
Who wrote that?

00:42:51.820 --> 00:42:52.430
Not me.

00:42:52.430 --> 00:42:53.250
Yeah.

00:42:53.250 --> 00:42:55.450
This is super simple to use.

00:42:55.450 --> 00:42:56.690
And it's now secured.

00:42:56.690 --> 00:42:59.860
My Go code has not changed yet.

00:42:59.860 --> 00:43:01.990
So how do we make this work?

00:43:01.990 --> 00:43:04.090
I want to call that API.

00:43:04.090 --> 00:43:10.190
So the way it's going to
work is I need to go to here.

00:43:10.190 --> 00:43:16.460
And I'm going to
create an API manager.

00:43:16.460 --> 00:43:18.710
I'm going to go to credentials.

00:43:18.710 --> 00:43:21.836
We're going to
create a new API key.

00:43:21.836 --> 00:43:24.860
We're going to copy here.

00:43:24.860 --> 00:43:29.820
Go back and say,
key equals that.

00:43:29.820 --> 00:43:30.840
I'm going to send it.

00:43:30.840 --> 00:43:32.770
And now it works.

00:43:32.770 --> 00:43:34.660
And this is pretty
cool, because it

00:43:34.660 --> 00:43:39.070
means that anyone can go
and create a new API key

00:43:39.070 --> 00:43:42.740
and start using it
and be identified.

00:43:42.740 --> 00:43:45.867
So make sure--

00:43:45.867 --> 00:43:47.700
I'm not following the
script at all anymore.

00:43:47.700 --> 00:43:50.241
And for the demos I want to make
sure I don't forget anything

00:43:50.241 --> 00:43:52.291
that I want to tell you.

00:43:52.291 --> 00:43:52.790
Cool.

00:43:52.790 --> 00:43:53.520
So, yeah.

00:43:53.520 --> 00:43:55.400
This now works.

00:43:55.400 --> 00:44:00.200
Our next step is, OK,
so API keys are cool,

00:44:00.200 --> 00:44:03.030
but are they actually safe?

00:44:03.030 --> 00:44:04.942
There's a very good
document written on this,

00:44:04.942 --> 00:44:07.150
on documentation that we
published not that long ago.

00:44:07.150 --> 00:44:08.710
And you can read it.

00:44:08.710 --> 00:44:12.330
But the interesting thing
about it is that they are not,

00:44:12.330 --> 00:44:13.560
basically.

00:44:13.560 --> 00:44:16.200
An API key has a big problem,
which is you sending it

00:44:16.200 --> 00:44:17.980
with every single request.

00:44:17.980 --> 00:44:22.110
So you're sending an API
key over HTTP in a URL.

00:44:22.110 --> 00:44:23.840
That's going to get
logged somewhere.

00:44:23.840 --> 00:44:25.120
Everyone's going to find it.

00:44:25.120 --> 00:44:27.180
You're out of luck.

00:44:27.180 --> 00:44:29.760
You could also put it in
your HTML if you want to.

00:44:29.760 --> 00:44:31.920
And now everyone
can have it too.

00:44:31.920 --> 00:44:33.481
So it is not super safe.

00:44:33.481 --> 00:44:34.980
But when you're
doing authentication

00:44:34.980 --> 00:44:38.490
across different projects, like
a client that has an API key.

00:44:38.490 --> 00:44:41.020
This is super simple
and very simple to use.

00:44:41.020 --> 00:44:43.560
You don't need to go
overkill with OAuth 2

00:44:43.560 --> 00:44:45.910
if you actually trust
what you're doing.

00:44:45.910 --> 00:44:48.180
If you have the binary
running somewhere,

00:44:48.180 --> 00:44:51.510
it can use an API
key quite safely.

00:44:51.510 --> 00:44:55.490
But for other cases,
what do we do?

00:44:55.490 --> 00:44:59.780
Well, so as we said,
there's the API key

00:44:59.780 --> 00:45:01.730
that you can use with
the API producer,

00:45:01.730 --> 00:45:05.580
the API producer
being my echo server.

00:45:05.580 --> 00:45:08.780
But also, if you have a
user that wants to use this,

00:45:08.780 --> 00:45:10.460
very often what you're
going to have is

00:45:10.460 --> 00:45:13.350
the end user is going to use
some kind of authentication.

00:45:13.350 --> 00:45:17.090
It's going to obtain an
authentication token.

00:45:17.090 --> 00:45:19.610
And they're going to send
that authentication token

00:45:19.610 --> 00:45:20.990
to my back-end.

00:45:20.990 --> 00:45:24.350
The ESP, the Endpoints--

00:45:24.350 --> 00:45:25.850
I never remember
what it stands for,

00:45:25.850 --> 00:45:29.355
the S. I think it's extensible,
which doesn't make any sense--

00:45:29.355 --> 00:45:33.052
the Endpoints
Extensible Proxy will

00:45:33.052 --> 00:45:34.760
take care of that
authentication for you,

00:45:34.760 --> 00:45:36.410
too, to verify that
this is actually

00:45:36.410 --> 00:45:39.580
sending some authentication.

00:45:39.580 --> 00:45:43.840
So for that we can
use Firebase or Auth0.

00:45:43.840 --> 00:45:45.730
Also you can use
Jason Web Tokens.

00:45:45.730 --> 00:45:48.380
Jason Web Tokens doesn't
require extra steps, actually,

00:45:48.380 --> 00:45:49.850
your back-end can generate it.

00:45:49.850 --> 00:45:52.600
Firebase and Auth, too, I
think they're very nice.

00:45:52.600 --> 00:45:54.250
Because what they
allow you to do

00:45:54.250 --> 00:45:57.550
is to have third party
authentication, which

00:45:57.550 --> 00:46:01.390
means that you don't need to
care about what authentication

00:46:01.390 --> 00:46:02.170
you allow.

00:46:02.170 --> 00:46:03.940
It's very easy to
say, I want people

00:46:03.940 --> 00:46:05.500
to authenticate with
Google, but also

00:46:05.500 --> 00:46:09.190
with Facebook, and Twitter, and
Yahoo, and every other ones.

00:46:09.190 --> 00:46:11.230
Your code does not
really change with this.

00:46:11.230 --> 00:46:14.050
What you're doing is you're
changing the code on the client

00:46:14.050 --> 00:46:15.190
to obtain that token.

00:46:15.190 --> 00:46:17.180
That token will be from
a different origin.

00:46:17.180 --> 00:46:19.221
But your code in the
back-end will be pretty much

00:46:19.221 --> 00:46:22.000
the same, which is pretty nice.

00:46:22.000 --> 00:46:23.280
So how do you do it?

00:46:23.280 --> 00:46:26.700
In Firebase, you just create a
Firebase security definition.

00:46:26.700 --> 00:46:27.200
That's it.

00:46:29.720 --> 00:46:32.490
With Auth0, same
thing-- you just

00:46:32.490 --> 00:46:37.090
create an Auth0
security definition.

00:46:37.090 --> 00:46:38.210
And then you redeploy.

00:46:38.210 --> 00:46:40.543
And if you want to know more
about authentication, which

00:46:40.543 --> 00:46:42.910
is actually a very interesting
topic, specifically

00:46:42.910 --> 00:46:46.750
how to authenticate
different services,

00:46:46.750 --> 00:46:52.172
like service-to-service, there's
a session tomorrow from 2:40.

00:46:52.172 --> 00:46:54.130
That's what I think, but
that might be changed.

00:46:54.130 --> 00:46:56.338
I've heard that schedules
are switching a little bit.

00:46:56.338 --> 00:46:57.520
So pay attention to that.

00:46:57.520 --> 00:46:59.470
It might be a little
bit later maybe.

00:46:59.470 --> 00:47:02.470
And we're going to have two of
the people from the Endpoints

00:47:02.470 --> 00:47:05.510
team talking about what are
the best practices to do

00:47:05.510 --> 00:47:07.360
authentication across services.

00:47:07.360 --> 00:47:09.406
So if you're interested
in that, go check it out,

00:47:09.406 --> 00:47:10.780
Service-to-service
Authentication

00:47:10.780 --> 00:47:13.560
in Cloud Endpoints.

00:47:13.560 --> 00:47:15.220
OK.

00:47:15.220 --> 00:47:16.780
Last part, we said
that we're going

00:47:16.780 --> 00:47:18.520
to share the API to the world.

00:47:18.520 --> 00:47:20.500
We have not shared that yet.

00:47:20.500 --> 00:47:23.290
The only person that is allowed
to use it is anyone that has

00:47:23.290 --> 00:47:28.510
access to my project, my
Cloud Endpoints Next project,

00:47:28.510 --> 00:47:32.350
that project ID on my personal--
well, not personal account--

00:47:32.350 --> 00:47:35.890
on my Go account, which
is not good enough.

00:47:35.890 --> 00:47:39.100
I want to expose
that API to anyone.

00:47:39.100 --> 00:47:40.310
How do I do that?

00:47:40.310 --> 00:47:42.650
Well, there's a couple
of ways of doing it.

00:47:42.650 --> 00:47:44.561
One of the ways--

00:47:44.561 --> 00:47:46.060
I'll get to the
message in a minute.

00:47:46.060 --> 00:47:47.890
I thought I had a slide there.

00:47:47.890 --> 00:47:50.260
One of the ways is by email.

00:47:50.260 --> 00:47:53.450
I can say, this person with
this email is allowed to use it.

00:47:53.450 --> 00:47:58.240
So now what you can do is any
project where that email is

00:47:58.240 --> 00:48:03.250
authorized to use it, they
are able to enable that API.

00:48:03.250 --> 00:48:05.240
So that is a way of
doing it, by email.

00:48:05.240 --> 00:48:11.020
You can also do it by Google
Mail Groups, so Google Groups.

00:48:11.020 --> 00:48:12.880
You can create a
Google Group and say,

00:48:12.880 --> 00:48:16.187
any address that is in that
Google Group, any project that

00:48:16.187 --> 00:48:18.520
is part of that address that
is part of the Google Group

00:48:18.520 --> 00:48:20.830
will have access to this API.

00:48:20.830 --> 00:48:22.420
So you can do--

00:48:22.420 --> 00:48:26.509
you can start doing this quite
simply, sharing with only

00:48:26.509 --> 00:48:27.800
the people that you care about.

00:48:32.090 --> 00:48:35.410
So how do we do that?

00:48:35.410 --> 00:48:37.709
So what I'm going to
be doing is I'm going

00:48:37.709 --> 00:48:39.000
to have two different accounts.

00:48:39.000 --> 00:48:42.390
I have my @golang.org
account, and I also

00:48:42.390 --> 00:48:43.390
have my Gmail account.

00:48:43.390 --> 00:48:44.931
And what I'm going
to do is I'm going

00:48:44.931 --> 00:48:48.120
to share that API with myself,
because I'm very generous.

00:48:48.120 --> 00:48:50.600
And then I'm going to
enable it and call it

00:48:50.600 --> 00:48:52.800
with an API key
that is generated

00:48:52.800 --> 00:48:54.540
from my personal account.

00:48:54.540 --> 00:48:55.350
OK.

00:48:55.350 --> 00:49:06.320
So first step, we're going
to go here to our Endpoints

00:49:06.320 --> 00:49:08.640
right there.

00:49:08.640 --> 00:49:10.490
This is the one.

00:49:10.490 --> 00:49:13.300
We're going to
click on share API.

00:49:13.300 --> 00:49:15.340
And I'm going to just--

00:49:15.340 --> 00:49:17.630
OK, I'm going to remove it.

00:49:17.630 --> 00:49:19.150
I'm going to share
it with myself.

00:49:19.150 --> 00:49:22.280
So I'm going to share it
with campoy83@gmail.com.

00:49:22.280 --> 00:49:23.470
Now you know how old I am.

00:49:26.620 --> 00:49:29.770
And now I have shared that API.

00:49:29.770 --> 00:49:31.180
What's next?

00:49:31.180 --> 00:49:34.600
I'm going to log in with
my personal account, which

00:49:34.600 --> 00:49:38.720
if I'm not completely
mistaken is here.

00:49:38.720 --> 00:49:39.220
OK.

00:49:39.220 --> 00:49:40.290
That is my personal account.

00:49:40.290 --> 00:49:42.290
You can see it's a
completely different picture.

00:49:42.290 --> 00:49:43.210
That's the proof.

00:49:43.210 --> 00:49:47.410
And now what I can see is
I can go to API manager.

00:49:47.410 --> 00:49:50.800
It's one more API,
nothing crazy here.

00:49:50.800 --> 00:49:53.110
You enable the same way
you enabled a Compute

00:49:53.110 --> 00:49:56.420
API, you enable any other API.

00:49:56.420 --> 00:50:00.090
I'm going to click
on enable API.

00:50:00.090 --> 00:50:04.260
And then you have that
Private APIs right there,

00:50:04.260 --> 00:50:06.960
which I'm very
thankful it's working.

00:50:06.960 --> 00:50:10.500
And here you can see
echo-api.endpoints--

00:50:10.500 --> 00:50:13.780
oh, this one here-- echo
api.endpoints.cloud next

00:50:13.780 --> 00:50:15.960
endpoints.cloud.goog, which
is the name of the API

00:50:15.960 --> 00:50:17.520
that we had.

00:50:17.520 --> 00:50:18.990
You click there.

00:50:18.990 --> 00:50:21.480
You're going to see
documentation a little bit.

00:50:21.480 --> 00:50:23.640
You can enable it.

00:50:23.640 --> 00:50:27.070
And when you enable
it, it says, would you

00:50:27.070 --> 00:50:28.330
like to create credentials?

00:50:28.330 --> 00:50:29.955
Which you should,
because otherwise you

00:50:29.955 --> 00:50:32.170
will not be able to call it.

00:50:32.170 --> 00:50:35.250
So now I can create--

00:50:35.250 --> 00:50:36.360
let's say, other API.

00:50:41.530 --> 00:50:42.740
What?

00:50:42.740 --> 00:50:43.240
OK.

00:50:43.240 --> 00:50:44.990
Let me do it again.

00:50:44.990 --> 00:50:46.800
Create an API key.

00:50:46.800 --> 00:50:49.980
I'm going to get that one.

00:50:49.980 --> 00:50:53.270
And now if I'm here, let's try--

00:50:53.270 --> 00:50:57.990
with some API key that is not
allowed, it does not work.

00:50:57.990 --> 00:51:02.220
With API key that is
allowed that I created--

00:51:02.220 --> 00:51:03.570
I copy/pasted wrongly I think.

00:51:12.750 --> 00:51:13.250
Copy.

00:51:18.580 --> 00:51:20.340
That is the same.

00:51:20.340 --> 00:51:22.420
No.

00:51:22.420 --> 00:51:24.460
I did something wrong.

00:51:24.460 --> 00:51:25.820
OK.

00:51:25.820 --> 00:51:29.390
What I'm going to do is
I'm going to fake it.

00:51:29.390 --> 00:51:31.200
So I'm going to go here.

00:51:31.200 --> 00:51:35.000
I'm going to go
to my credentials.

00:51:35.000 --> 00:51:38.280
Credentials, my API key.

00:51:38.280 --> 00:51:39.420
I'm going to put it there.

00:51:39.420 --> 00:51:42.820
And you can imagine that that
is actually a different one,

00:51:42.820 --> 00:51:44.610
and that this actually worked.

00:51:44.610 --> 00:51:45.110
Boom.

00:51:45.110 --> 00:51:45.609
Done.

00:51:45.609 --> 00:51:47.080
Yeah.

00:51:47.080 --> 00:51:47.580
OK.

00:51:47.580 --> 00:51:49.140
So the whole idea--

00:51:49.140 --> 00:51:51.570
I'm sure I missed some
steps in the demo--

00:51:51.570 --> 00:51:55.290
but the whole idea is that
the API key creation now

00:51:55.290 --> 00:51:57.000
is not controlled by me.

00:51:57.000 --> 00:51:58.310
It's controlled by Google.

00:51:58.310 --> 00:52:00.780
But I do decide who
has access to what.

00:52:00.780 --> 00:52:04.200
And also, when those calls are
done, now they are monitored.

00:52:04.200 --> 00:52:06.782
And you're actually logging
who is accessing what.

00:52:06.782 --> 00:52:08.490
And if you want to
remove that access you

00:52:08.490 --> 00:52:11.310
can easily do it
by revoking the--

00:52:11.310 --> 00:52:13.740
I'm sharing that
API with someone.

00:52:13.740 --> 00:52:17.550
So all of a sudden you have
all of this functionality.

00:52:17.550 --> 00:52:21.810
And my code is still the same,
which that makes me happy.

00:52:25.190 --> 00:52:28.510
So what else can you do
with Cloud Endpoints?

00:52:28.510 --> 00:52:30.510
There's actually lots
of really cool stuff.

00:52:30.510 --> 00:52:32.650
If you have never used gRPC--

00:52:32.650 --> 00:52:33.910
I love gRPC.

00:52:33.910 --> 00:52:36.240
I think it's way
better than the REST.

00:52:36.240 --> 00:52:39.590
But it's the little problem
that gRPC's amazing.

00:52:39.590 --> 00:52:40.870
It's way better than REST.

00:52:40.870 --> 00:52:42.230
But people love REST.

00:52:42.230 --> 00:52:45.040
So you need to expose
REST APIs, right?

00:52:45.040 --> 00:52:48.760
There's a really cool thing
called the gRPC gateway.

00:52:48.760 --> 00:52:53.410
And gRPC gateway is a project
that is pretty popular now.

00:52:53.410 --> 00:52:56.800
What it does is you give
it a gRPC specification.

00:52:56.800 --> 00:53:00.315
And from there it
generates a REST API,

00:53:00.315 --> 00:53:02.440
which means that you can
have both at the same time

00:53:02.440 --> 00:53:04.770
without writing the
documentation or anything

00:53:04.770 --> 00:53:05.532
twice.

00:53:05.532 --> 00:53:06.490
This is pretty amazing.

00:53:06.490 --> 00:53:08.710
And this is actually
something very similar to what

00:53:08.710 --> 00:53:10.480
we do internally at Google.

00:53:10.480 --> 00:53:12.190
If you see Google
APIs very often

00:53:12.190 --> 00:53:14.960
come in two flavors--
gRPC and REST.

00:53:14.960 --> 00:53:17.090
What they do is
pretty much the same.

00:53:17.090 --> 00:53:18.130
We generate the gRPC.

00:53:18.130 --> 00:53:19.180
We write gRPC.

00:53:19.180 --> 00:53:20.290
The gRPC is the fast one.

00:53:20.290 --> 00:53:21.340
It's the amazing one.

00:53:21.340 --> 00:53:24.550
And REST is the one that
is there for convenience

00:53:24.550 --> 00:53:26.390
and very often is fast enough.

00:53:26.390 --> 00:53:28.960
But if you're doing things
like Bigtable, Spanner, things

00:53:28.960 --> 00:53:31.690
like that, the fact
that you're using JSON

00:53:31.690 --> 00:53:33.640
and you're encoding,
decoding that JSON,

00:53:33.640 --> 00:53:36.010
it's expensive enough
that you might consider

00:53:36.010 --> 00:53:39.070
going to gRPC directly.

00:53:39.070 --> 00:53:40.620
And then that logo--

00:53:40.620 --> 00:53:41.320
Cloud Functions.

00:53:41.320 --> 00:53:42.236
We're working on that.

00:53:42.236 --> 00:53:44.710
There's a lot of people asking
for this, Cloud Functions.

00:53:44.710 --> 00:53:47.314
I think it was announced
today that they're now beta.

00:53:47.314 --> 00:53:47.980
I'm almost sure.

00:53:47.980 --> 00:53:50.350
And if not, I just
announced something for you.

00:53:50.350 --> 00:53:52.320
I'm almost sure
it was announced.

00:53:52.320 --> 00:53:55.480
But it's now beta, so
you can go play with it.

00:53:55.480 --> 00:53:57.570
And Cloud Endpoints
for Cloud Functions

00:53:57.570 --> 00:53:59.232
will be there at some point.

00:53:59.232 --> 00:54:00.190
So we're working on it.

00:54:00.190 --> 00:54:02.560
And I'm very excited about
this, because the experience

00:54:02.560 --> 00:54:05.639
of having serverless
plus not having

00:54:05.639 --> 00:54:07.180
to write any code
for authentication,

00:54:07.180 --> 00:54:10.270
logging, or monitoring,
is going to be amazing.

00:54:10.270 --> 00:54:13.180
And with that I'm going
to finish because there's

00:54:13.180 --> 00:54:15.610
much, much more to learn.

00:54:15.610 --> 00:54:18.040
The cool thing is that
we're just starting.

00:54:18.040 --> 00:54:21.130
Cloud Endpoints is
a very new project.

00:54:21.130 --> 00:54:25.240
And we have basically shown
what our idea is, what

00:54:25.240 --> 00:54:27.040
is the core technology of this.

00:54:27.040 --> 00:54:28.430
But we are far from done.

00:54:28.430 --> 00:54:31.550
We are going to be adding
more and more features.

00:54:31.550 --> 00:54:34.240
So if you have features
in mind, let us know.

00:54:34.240 --> 00:54:35.902
We're always looking for--

00:54:35.902 --> 00:54:38.110
as I said at the beginning,
I'm a Developer Advocate.

00:54:38.110 --> 00:54:40.430
My goal is to make
developers happy.

00:54:40.430 --> 00:54:43.300
So if you have an idea on
how I could make you happy.

00:54:43.300 --> 00:54:44.440
Let me know.

00:54:44.440 --> 00:54:45.040
Thank you.

00:54:45.040 --> 00:54:45.940
[APPLAUSE]

00:54:45.940 --> 00:54:48.690
[MUSIC PLAYING]

